  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}|{X}_{i}|$\n- $min({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{13},{X}_{15})$\n- $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15},\\sigma )$",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only.\n\n- Expression II is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
